-- Curated Keep TX Red article for 2026-08-07.
WITH seed AS (
  SELECT
    '2026-08-07-texas-hurricane-odds-drop-el-nino'::text AS slug,
    'Non-Political'::text AS category,
    'Texas Hurricane Odds Drop Sharply as El Niño Strengthens — but Peak Season Is Still Ahead'::text AS title,
    'Colorado State University cut its estimate of a Texas hurricane impact from 27% in April to 13% in August as stronger El Niño conditions disrupt Atlantic storms.'::text AS dek,
    'Houston Chronicle'::text AS source_name,
    'https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-weather/hurricanes/article/texas-hurricane-risk-drops-new-outlook-22376497.php'::text AS source_url,
    '2026-08-07T12:13:00Z'::timestamptz AS published_at,
    24::int AS score,
    FALSE::boolean AS is_breaking,
    '{"updated":"2026-08-07","intro":["Texas’ odds of a hurricane impact have fallen substantially since spring as forecasters gain confidence that a strengthening El Niño will make the Atlantic less favorable for tropical development. The improved outlook is welcome news for the Gulf Coast, but meteorologists caution that August and September remain the heart of hurricane season and a lower probability is not the same as no risk."],"sections":[{"heading":"How the Texas odds changed","paragraphs":["Colorado State University researchers estimated in April that Texas had a 27% chance of a hurricane passing within 50 miles of the coast during the season. By the August update, that estimate had fallen to 13%. The projected chance of a major hurricane impact also declined, from 11% to 5%, according to the Chronicle’s summary of the seasonal outlook.","Those numbers are probabilistic, not a promise about what will happen at a specific city. A statewide coastal probability blends a long shoreline and many possible storm tracks. A single hurricane can still produce catastrophic losses even during a below-normal season, which is why emergency managers continue to urge residents to maintain plans and supplies."]},{"heading":"Why El Niño matters","paragraphs":["El Niño is a warming pattern in the tropical Pacific that can change wind patterns far beyond that ocean basin. In the Atlantic, a stronger El Niño often increases vertical wind shear — changes in wind speed or direction with height — that can tilt or disrupt developing tropical cyclones. Storms generally need a more vertically aligned structure to intensify efficiently.","Forecasters became more confident through the summer that El Niño would be strong enough to suppress some Atlantic activity. That confidence drove a reduction in the expected number of named storms, hurricanes and major hurricanes. The seasonal environment now looks less favorable than it did in the spring, when forecasts had to account for greater uncertainty about Pacific conditions."]},{"heading":"The season has already produced Texas rain","paragraphs":["Texas has already dealt with tropical systems this year. Tropical Storm Arthur in June and Tropical Storm Bertha in July brought heavy rain even though neither became a hurricane at the Texas coast. Those storms are reminders that wind category alone does not determine impact. Slow movement, moisture and local drainage conditions can make a weaker system dangerous.","Flooding is particularly important after the destructive rain events Texas has experienced in recent years. Gulf moisture can aggravate river flooding or overwhelm urban drainage even when a storm’s center remains weak. Residents far from the immediate coastline should therefore pay attention to rainfall forecasts, not just hurricane-cone graphics."]},{"heading":"Why August and September still matter","paragraphs":["The historical peak of Atlantic hurricane activity occurs after the start of August as ocean temperatures are warm and large areas of the tropics become capable of supporting long-lived storms. Some of Texas’ most damaging hurricanes have occurred during this late-summer period. Seasonal suppression from El Niño lowers the overall odds but does not eliminate windows when conditions can briefly become favorable.","A storm only needs a few days of supportive conditions to intensify and threaten the Gulf. Forecast skill improves dramatically once a system forms, but long-range seasonal outlooks cannot identify the exact week or county that may be at risk. That is why preparedness recommendations remain largely unchanged even when seasonal totals are revised downward."]},{"heading":"What Gulf Coast residents should do","paragraphs":["Households should keep evacuation routes, medication, insurance records, pet plans and emergency supplies current. Flood insurance deserves special attention because policies can include waiting periods before coverage begins. Waiting until a storm is in the Gulf may be too late to add protection for the approaching event.","Residents should also distinguish official watches and warnings from social-media model screenshots. Individual computer-model runs can shift hundreds of miles and are not forecasts by themselves. The National Hurricane Center, local National Weather Service offices and emergency-management agencies remain the best sources for decisions about evacuation and immediate safety."]},{"heading":"The practical takeaway","paragraphs":["The new outlook is genuinely better for Texas than the spring forecast. A drop from 27% to 13% in the modeled hurricane-impact probability is meaningful, and the stronger El Niño gives forecasters a physical reason for the change rather than a simple statistical adjustment. It is reasonable for Texans to view the seasonal threat as lower than it appeared in April.","But the remaining 13% is not trivial when the consequence can be a major coastal disaster. Preparedness has value even if no hurricane arrives because many supplies and plans are useful during other emergencies. The best interpretation of the August update is reduced risk, not permission to ignore the Gulf during the busiest stretch of the season.","Residents should also remember that a seasonal probability is not a forecast for a specific storm. A lower Texas percentage does not tell any one household whether it will be affected, and a single landfall can define an otherwise quiet season for the communities in its path. The most useful response is therefore proportional rather than complacent: acknowledge that the background odds improved, keep routine hurricane preparations in place, and pay attention when an actual tropical system forms. Forecast skill improves dramatically once meteorologists are tracking a real storm with a defined circulation, projected path and intensity."]},{"heading":"Source attribution","paragraphs":["This article is an independent Keep TX Red rewrite based on reporting from Houston Chronicle. The original report is linked below for verification."]}],"faq":[],"sources":[{"label":"Houston Chronicle — original report","url":"https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-weather/hurricanes/article/texas-hurricane-risk-drops-new-outlook-22376497.php"}],"keyTakeaways":["Colorado State University cut its estimate of a Texas hurricane impact from 27% in April to 13% in August as stronger El Niño conditions disrupt Atlantic storms."]}'::jsonb AS body_json
), prepared AS (
  SELECT seed.*,
    (SELECT string_agg(value, ' ') FROM jsonb_array_elements_text(
      jsonb_path_query_array(seed.body_json, '$.intro[*]') ||
      jsonb_path_query_array(seed.body_json, '$.sections[*].paragraphs[*]')
    )) AS body_text
  FROM seed
)
INSERT INTO public.daily_articles
  (slug, internal_url, is_ingested, category, title, dek, source_name, source_url,
   published_at, score, is_breaking, kind, body, body_json)
SELECT slug, '/news/' || slug, FALSE, category, title, dek, source_name, source_url,
       published_at, score, is_breaking, 'news', body_text, body_json
FROM prepared
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  internal_url=EXCLUDED.internal_url, is_ingested=EXCLUDED.is_ingested,
  category=EXCLUDED.category, title=EXCLUDED.title, dek=EXCLUDED.dek,
  source_name=EXCLUDED.source_name, source_url=EXCLUDED.source_url,
  published_at=EXCLUDED.published_at, score=EXCLUDED.score,
  is_breaking=EXCLUDED.is_breaking, kind=EXCLUDED.kind,
  body=EXCLUDED.body, body_json=EXCLUDED.body_json;
